# TSX conventions

Prefer functional components. Pass props for shallow trees (one or two levels). Introduce React context only when it removes real props drilling — several nested consumers sharing the same state or actions.

Do not create a new context for every subtree. Before adding one, check whether an existing context already serves the same purpose and can be extended. If a context grows into unrelated concerns, split it rather than stuffing more into the value.

Colocate the provider, typed value, and a hook that throws when used outside the provider. Prefer focused consumer hooks over exposing the entire value everywhere.

```tsx
// ❌ BAD — new context for a one-level prop, or a second context for the same form
function ProductFields({ model, updateModel }: { model: ModelDraft; updateModel: ... }) { ... }

// ❌ BAD — kitchen-sink context mixing unrelated features
type AppContextValue = { productForm: ...; warehouseStock: ...; theme: ... };

// ✅ GOOD — one feature context; nested fields read it instead of threading props
function ModelFields() {
  const { model, updateModel, fieldErrors } = useCreateProductForm();
  // ...
}

function useProductDraft(productId: string) {
  const { products, updateProduct } = useCreateProductForm();
  // scoped slice; same context, not a new one
}
```

Reference: `CreateProductFormProvider` / `useCreateProductForm` in product create. `WarehouseStockView` passing a few props to nearby children is fine — no context needed.
